# Preparing for Reinforcement Learning

The scaling experiment ended with an intention:

Stop patching heuristics and look at algorithms built for constraint satisfaction and information gain;

reinforcement learning among them.

Before building anything new, the existing system got a hard look first.

> A learning agent trained on top of a flawed environment won't reveal the flaw.

> It will just learn to exploit it.

---

## What I Found in Between

A few issues had been sitting quietly in the existing code:

* The Hybrid Agent was marking every candidate it merely considered as tried, not just the one it submitted.

  It was silently shrinking its own future search space every step;

  and possibly providing a second, independent explanation for why it lost to the Logic Agent at scale.

* Several agents relied on unbounded search loops.

  It was harmless at the current scale, but a real liability once similar logic would need to run unattended, potentially millions of times inside a training loop.

* The survival curve plots conflated a genuine last-step solve with a plain timeout.

  This made every agent's curve misleadingly hit 100% at the final step.

Each of these mattered less as isolated bugs and more as a signal:

the project's foundation needed to be trustworthy before a learning agent was allowed to train on top of it.

---

## Reframing the Puzzle

With that settled, the real design question was how to describe this puzzle in terms an RL algorithm understands.

The secret is never observed directly, only a single number per guess;

the same **"global constraint, not local evidence" property the scaling experiment had already learned the hard way.

Whatever the RL agent would see needed to respect that same lesson, not quietly reintroduce it.

The harder problem was the action space.

The obvious action, submitting a full permutation creates a Discrete(n!) output space, which explodes exactly the way the search space itself does.

Three ways of shrinking it were considered:

* build the guess step by step,

* let the environment offer a pool of candidates to choose from,

* or restrict the action to a single swap on the current best guess.

The swap option was chosen first:

a small, ordinary action space, and a direct generalization of what the Logic Agent already does by hand, just with the choice of swap handed to a learned policy instead of random swaps.

Reward design carried the same caution as the observation.

Shaping reward directly from the guess to guess score change would have been the most tempting option and;

on reflection the exact mistake already unlearned once before:

rewarding something that looks like progress but isn't necessarily reliable evidence of it.

Reward was kept simple instead:

a small penalty per step, and a bonus on solving that rewards getting there faster.

With the foundation checked and the problem reframed in RL terms, the next step was to actually build it.
